{"answer":"The evidence did not separate staffing level from order volume as the better explanation for on-time rate. Both claims were weakened rather than supported, so neither driver clearly won.

On-time rate was associated with staffing level, but that claim was weakened, with 6 supporting evidence items and no refuting evidence. On-time rate was also associated with order volume, and that claim was weakened in the same way, with 6 supporting evidence items and no refuting evidence. The run tested the relationships with a correlation analysis and then stopped because there was insufficient evidence to decide between them.

Three questions remained open: which driver showed the stronger association with on-time rate, whether either relationship held after accounting for the other variable, and which explanation the evidence actually favoured. The dataset was operational_delivery with 96 rows."}

